id: SCA-078
ilities: [integrity]
maturity: 2
title: Install/shell script downloads a binary without checksum verification
category: supply-chain
severity: info
engine: absence
# Companion to SCA-030 (Makefiles) and SCA-063 (curl|sh): a shell script that
# fetches a release artifact over the network should verify its integrity
# (sha256) BEFORE trusting it. This rule targets scripts that clearly download
# (curl/wget to an http(s) URL) but never mention a checksum tool/file. It is
# `absence`-based: the finding fires when the download pattern is present but no
# verification pattern is. info-level like SCA-030 — a nudge, not a blocker —
# because static matching cannot prove the hash is actually compared, only that
# the machinery is referenced.
#
# A correct installer (download → sha256sum -c → install) mentions sha256sum /
# shasum / SHA256SUMS and so does NOT fire. keur's own install.sh is the worked
# example. @see rules/supply-chain/SCA-030, SCA-063.
target:
  extensions: .sh .bash
  filenames: install.sh install install.bash
  exclude_paths: test/ tests/ node_modules/ .git/ vendor/
  content_contains: "http"
patterns:
  # Require evidence of integrity checking somewhere in the file; its ABSENCE
  # (when the file downloads) is the finding.
  - regex: (sha256sum|shasum|SHA256SUMS|sha256:-c|gpg --verify|cosign verify|minisign)
    message: "This script downloads over the network but shows no checksum/signature verification. A compromised server, CDN, or MITM can deliver a malicious binary. Download first, verify sha256 against a committed SHA256SUMS, then install."
fix: "Fetch the artifact AND a SHA256SUMS file, compare with sha256sum/shasum before using it (download → verify → install). See keur's own install.sh for the pattern."
